1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,850
Radio message: "Theo, Heinrich here. Have shot down two bombers."

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:06,100
"Out of ammunition. I'm ramming now."

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:09,100
"Goodbye, see you in Valhalla."

4
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:13,757
That is said to have been Heinrich Ehrler's last radio

5
00:00:13,757 --> 00:00:18,030
message, in April 1945, in the sky over Stendal.

6
00:00:18,470 --> 00:00:22,990
A comrade claimed to have heard it over the loudspeaker in the command post.

7
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:26,430
Whether those were really the words, nobody knows today.

8
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:28,140
Only this is certain:

9
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:30,620
A few weeks before the end of the war, one of the

10
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,650
Luftwaffe's most successful fighter pilots died.

11
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:36,854
And just five months earlier he had been blamed

12
00:00:36,854 --> 00:00:40,690
for one of the German navy's greatest disasters.

13
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:43,190
This is his story.

14
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:47,530
Heinrich Ehrler was born on 14 September 1917 in

15
00:00:47,530 --> 00:00:51,790
Oberbalbach, a small village in northern Baden.

16
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:56,850
He grew up in a large working-class family and trained as a butcher.

17
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:00,350
In 1935, at 18, he joined the Wehrmacht, first

18
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:03,080
the artillery, then the anti-aircraft branch.

19
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:06,579
At the end of 1936 he went to Spain with the

20
00:01:06,579 --> 00:01:10,520
Condor Legion, into the Spanish Civil War.

21
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:13,330
For almost a year he manned anti-aircraft guns

22
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:16,450
there and only saw the planes from below.

23
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:18,750
But he wanted to fly himself.

24
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:24,010
In January 1940 he applied for a transfer to the fighter arm.

25
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:27,783
In February 1941 he joined Jagdgeschwader 77 in

26
00:01:27,783 --> 00:01:32,140
Stavanger as a young lieutenant, on Norway's west coast.

27
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:37,513
In May 1942 he was sent even further north, to the 6th Staffel

28
00:01:37,513 --> 00:01:43,350
of Jagdgeschwader 5 in Petsamo, in the far north of Finland.

29
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:46,430
It was called the Arctic Ocean Wing.

30
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:49,470
Up here the war was different.

31
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:55,010
In winter the sun did not rise for weeks, in summer it did not set for weeks.

32
00:01:55,510 --> 00:02:00,060
Snowstorms, fog and icy cold shaped every mission.

33
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:04,791
Not far away lay Murmansk, the ice-free port through which the

34
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:10,450
Allies shipped tanks, aircraft and ammunition to the Soviet Union.

35
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:16,750
In the sky the Messerschmitt Bf 109s met Soviet fighters, but also American P-40s

36
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:23,110
and British Hurricanes that had reached the Red Army through that very port.

37
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:26,490
Ehrler reported victory after victory.

38
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:29,842
In summer 1942 he became squadron leader, and

39
00:02:29,842 --> 00:02:33,650
on 4 September he received the Knight's Cross.

40
00:02:33,970 --> 00:02:39,370
On 8 June 1943 he reported his hundredth aerial victory.

41
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:43,210
He was only the fortieth Luftwaffe pilot to do so.

42
00:02:43,390 --> 00:02:46,150
But he was not invulnerable either.

43
00:02:46,270 --> 00:02:51,620
On 21 June 1943 he was shot down himself near Alakurtti.

44
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:55,800
He bailed out by parachute, behind Soviet lines.

45
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:59,290
That same day a seaplane brought him back.

46
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:06,840
In August 1943 he received the Oak Leaves to the Knight's Cross, for 112 victories.

47
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:10,570
That same year he took over an entire group of the wing.

48
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:16,280
In May 1944 he reported nine victories within 24 hours.

49
00:03:16,330 --> 00:03:24,390
On 1 August 1944 he became commander of Jagdgeschwader 5, at the age of 26.

50
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:28,010
But in autumn everything in the far north collapsed.

51
00:03:28,130 --> 00:03:32,180
In September Finland signed an armistice with the Soviet Union.

52
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:36,320
In October the Red Army overran Petsamo and Kirkenes.

53
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:41,040
The German troops withdrew to northern Norway, and the fighters with them.

54
00:03:41,090 --> 00:03:47,640
There, off the coast near Tromsø, lay the largest battleship Germany had ever built:

55
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:49,010
the Tirpitz.

56
00:03:49,210 --> 00:03:52,660
For years she had lain in Norway's fjords.

57
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:56,820
Her presence alone tied down British battleships in the Arctic.

58
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:02,690
In September 1944 a British Tallboy bomb hit her in Kåfjord.

59
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:07,460
A single hit wrecked her bow; she could no longer fight.

60
00:04:07,510 --> 00:04:10,560
Grand Admiral Dönitz had her moved to Tromsø.

61
00:04:10,610 --> 00:04:14,060
There she was to defend the coast as a floating battery.

62
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:16,860
In mid-October she anchored off the island of Håkøya.

63
00:04:16,910 --> 00:04:21,660
It was believed the water there was shallow enough to keep her from sinking.

64
00:04:21,710 --> 00:04:23,480
That was a mistake.

65
00:04:23,530 --> 00:04:27,940
On 29 October British Lancaster bombers attacked again.

66
00:04:27,990 --> 00:04:32,300
Just before the target clouds moved in, and no bomb hit directly.

67
00:04:32,350 --> 00:04:35,083
Afterwards fighters of Jagdgeschwader 5 were

68
00:04:35,083 --> 00:04:37,960
moved to Bardufoss, not far from Tromsø.

69
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:42,840
But the unit had only just withdrawn from Kirkenes ahead of the Red Army.

70
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:45,140
Many pilots were young and inexperienced.

71
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:51,100
And apparently no one had properly told them that the Tirpitz lay close by.

72
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:54,080
Ehrler arrived in Bardufoss on 9 November.

73
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:56,600
He was actually on his way to Alta.

74
00:04:56,650 --> 00:05:00,060
But he stayed to give the young pilots some quick training.

75
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:05,560
On the morning of 12 November 1944 the sky over Tromsø was clear.

76
00:05:05,610 --> 00:05:08,096
More than thirty Lancasters of British

77
00:05:08,096 --> 00:05:11,660
Squadrons 617 and 9 had taken off in Scotland.

78
00:05:11,710 --> 00:05:14,300
German observation posts saw the bombers coming.

79
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:18,080
But many believed they were flying on to the Soviet Union.

80
00:05:18,130 --> 00:05:22,340
The reports reached the Tirpitz late and Bardufoss barely at all.

81
00:05:22,390 --> 00:05:26,620
Only about a quarter of an hour before the attack were the fighters alerted.

82
00:05:26,670 --> 00:05:30,980
A Ju 52 that was just landing blocked the runway.

83
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:33,000
Ehrler took off first.

84
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:36,420
In the air he noticed that his radio was not transmitting.

85
00:05:36,470 --> 00:05:38,260
He could not reach anyone.

86
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:40,040
He did not find the bombers.

87
00:05:40,090 --> 00:05:42,980
From a distance he only saw a cloud of smoke over the fjord.

88
00:05:43,030 --> 00:05:49,700
Over Håkøya 29 Lancasters dropped their Tallboys, each weighing over five tons.

89
00:05:49,750 --> 00:05:51,940
Two hit the ship directly.

90
00:05:51,990 --> 00:05:55,360
The Tirpitz listed to port, further and further.

91
00:05:55,410 --> 00:05:57,722
An ammunition magazine exploded, and an

92
00:05:57,722 --> 00:06:00,940
entire gun turret was torn from its mounting.

93
00:06:00,990 --> 00:06:04,200
A few minutes after the hits the ship capsized.

94
00:06:04,250 --> 00:06:07,200
Hundreds of sailors were trapped inside the hull.

95
00:06:07,250 --> 00:06:11,020
Rescuers cut holes in the steel and pulled some out alive.

96
00:06:11,070 --> 00:06:14,580
But for most, any help came too late.

97
00:06:14,630 --> 00:06:19,920
Depending on the source, between about 900 and over 1,200 men died.

98
00:06:19,970 --> 00:06:22,680
The British did not lose a single man.

99
00:06:22,730 --> 00:06:25,320
Then the search for someone to blame began.

100
00:06:25,370 --> 00:06:27,420
Many had made mistakes.

101
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:30,215
Reports between the navy and the Luftwaffe only went

102
00:06:30,215 --> 00:06:33,930
through detours, and the anchorage was not shallow enough.

103
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:36,680
But soon one man was at the centre:

104
00:06:36,730 --> 00:06:41,298
the commander of Jagdgeschwader 5. In December 1944 Heinrich

105
00:06:41,298 --> 00:06:45,940
Ehrler stood before the Reich Military Court in Oslo.

106
00:06:45,990 --> 00:06:49,780
The charge of cowardice before the enemy was dropped.

107
00:06:49,830 --> 00:06:53,344
But the court accused him of leaving his command

108
00:06:53,344 --> 00:06:57,640
post to hunt for his two-hundredth victory himself.

109
00:06:57,690 --> 00:07:01,720
He was sentenced to three years in prison for disobedience.

110
00:07:01,770 --> 00:07:04,740
He lost his command and was demoted.

111
00:07:04,790 --> 00:07:07,160
Many later saw him as a scapegoat.

112
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:13,300
Comrades spoke up for him, among them Walter Schuck, a pilot of his wing.

113
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:15,460
The sentence was suspended.

114
00:07:15,510 --> 00:07:18,230
Ehrler was to prove himself at the front.

115
00:07:18,430 --> 00:07:21,417
At the end of February 1945 his friend Theodor

116
00:07:21,417 --> 00:07:24,500
Weissenberger brought him to his unit.

117
00:07:24,550 --> 00:07:27,877
Weissenberger commanded Jagdgeschwader 7, the

118
00:07:27,877 --> 00:07:31,730
world's first operational jet fighter unit.

119
00:07:31,850 --> 00:07:36,020
Its aircraft was the Messerschmitt Me 262.

120
00:07:36,070 --> 00:07:42,280
It flew over 850 kilometres per hour, faster than any Allied fighter.

121
00:07:42,330 --> 00:07:47,560
But there were too few of them, too little fuel and too few trained pilots.

122
00:07:47,610 --> 00:07:53,910
And on some days the Americans sent more than a thousand heavy bombers over Germany.

123
00:07:54,270 --> 00:08:02,610
On 21 March 1945 Ehrler flew his first jet mission and reported a B-17.

124
00:08:02,770 --> 00:08:07,840
A second followed the next day, two B-24s over Chemnitz

125
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:13,340
on 23 March, and another B-17 near Dessau on 24 March.

126
00:08:13,390 --> 00:08:18,210
In just four days he had scored five victories in the jet.

127
00:08:18,490 --> 00:08:21,130
Then came April 1945.

128
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:27,070
The Red Army stood on the Oder, the Western Allies had crossed the Rhine.

129
00:08:27,230 --> 00:08:30,614
On 4 April Ehrler took off with his wing's jets

130
00:08:30,614 --> 00:08:34,310
against a large American bomber formation.

131
00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:39,060
What happened next is known only from reports, and they contradict each other.

132
00:08:39,110 --> 00:08:43,060
According to the best-known version he shot down two bombers.

133
00:08:43,110 --> 00:08:45,260
Then his ammunition was gone.

134
00:08:45,310 --> 00:08:50,290
Walter Schuck later recalled hearing Ehrler's voice over the loudspeaker.

135
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:53,840
Radio message: "Theo, Heinrich here. Have shot down two bombers."

136
00:08:53,890 --> 00:08:56,090
"Out of ammunition. I'm ramming now."

137
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:59,090
"Goodbye, see you in Valhalla."

138
00:08:59,770 --> 00:09:03,830
Theo was Theodor Weissenberger, his commander.

139
00:09:04,050 --> 00:09:06,780
Others remembered different words.

140
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:10,730
Some sources even give 6 April as the day he died.

141
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:14,284
Whether Ehrler really rammed a bomber or was shot down

142
00:09:14,284 --> 00:09:17,540
by American escort fighters is still unclear today.

143
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:19,460
Only this is certain:

144
00:09:19,510 --> 00:09:23,830
His Me 262 crashed near Stendal.

145
00:09:23,930 --> 00:09:29,140
On 7 April a body was recovered and identified as Heinrich Ehrler.

146
00:09:29,190 --> 00:09:32,730
On 10 April he was buried in Stendal.

147
00:09:32,990 --> 00:09:36,260
Heinrich Ehrler was 27 years old.

148
00:09:36,310 --> 00:09:39,620
He is credited with 208 aerial victories, almost

149
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:42,880
all in the far north, eight of them in the jet.

150
00:09:42,930 --> 00:09:46,477
Some historians believe the real number is lower,

151
00:09:46,477 --> 00:09:50,190
because his wing's records are incomplete.

152
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:53,090
And even his grave holds a mystery.

153
00:09:53,210 --> 00:09:56,492
In 2018 researchers of missing soldiers found wreckage

154
00:09:56,492 --> 00:09:59,960
of his aircraft and human remains near Stendal.

155
00:10:00,010 --> 00:10:04,660
According to them, Ehrler does not lie in the grave that bears his name.

156
00:10:04,710 --> 00:10:08,320
A man who was blamed for the sinking of the Tirpitz.

157
00:10:08,370 --> 00:10:14,970
A pilot whose last words became a legend that no one can prove to this day.

158
00:10:15,030 --> 00:10:18,870
Five weeks after his death the war in Europe was over.
